window.MMQ_INDEX = [{"title": "Home", "url": "index.html", "text": "About MMQ MMQ (Multifunctional Meat Quality Determination Software) measures meat colour, marbling and intramuscular fat from cross-section images. It is written for Windows and is aimed at enterprise users. The default species is pig. A separate browser tool, PorkMS , grades pork marbling from fat area ratio without installing the desktop program. » Read more PorkMS Online Marbling Pork Marbling Measurement System runs in the browser. It reports fat ratio and a marbling grade. Images are processed locally and are not uploaded. » Read more Log-in window Start the desktop program as administrator. The initial account in the manual is username admin and password 1 . » Read more Main window Before a sample is measured, set the measurement mode, the scoring model and the measurement time. The status bar shows the current choices and the save path. » Read more Pig Species Pig Pig is the default species. Marbling score and intramuscular fat are measured on the 24-hour setting. Cattle Species Cattle Cattle can be chosen from the File menu. The measurement steps are the same as for pig. Sheep Species Sheep Sheep can be chosen from the File menu before a new information template is created. Chicken Species Chicken Chicken can be chosen from the File menu. PorkMS, the browser tool, is for pork marbling only."}, {"title": "About MMQ", "url": "about.html", "text": "MMQ is a Windows program for meat colour, marbling and intramuscular fat (IMF). Its English name is Multifunctional Meat Quality Determination Software. The window title identifies Version 1, © 2025 by NJAU (Nanjing Agricultural University). Pork is eaten widely, and its colour and eating quality matter to buyers. Intramuscular fat is one of the traits that affects pork flavour. Laboratory fat extraction, such as Soxhlet extraction, is done after slaughter and does not give breeders a quick measurement they can use while selecting stock. The desktop program is meant to score colour, marbling and IMF in one workflow. The people named in the software manual are Ji Qiunan, Liu Zedan, Wan Ziming, Zhao Sanqin and Wu Wangjun. The stated user is an enterprise. Fig. 7. Main window of MMQ. Desktop program and PorkMS Two tools are documented here. They both look at meat cross-sections, and they do not share one score scale. Tool Where it runs What it reports MMQ Windows desktop L*, a*, b*, a meat-colour score, a marbling score and IMF. The information table labels the colour and marbling scores as NPPC 6-point scores. The default model is NPPC. PorkMS Web browser Fat area ratio and a marbling grade from 1 to 10+. No account is required. Analysis stays in the browser. Do not copy an NPPC 6-point score into a PorkMS grade, or the reverse. Read the page for the program you are actually using. » How to use MMQ"}, {"title": "Download", "url": "download.html", "text": "MMQ, Version 1.0. MMQ Determination Software.exe User manual (PDF) Before using the software please read: Taking images Help How to start Double-click the executable, or right-click it and choose Run as administrator . The log-in window opens. The manual gives the initial username admin and the initial password 1 . See Log in . The printed manual also refers to the program file as the multifunctional meat-quality executable. In this distribution folder the file name is MMQ Determination Software.exe . Computer CPU: Intel Core i5 or higher Memory: 8 GB or more Discrete graphics memory: 2 GB or more A USB port Operating system 64-bit Windows 10 or Windows 11. The program writes templates to D:\\Meat_Quality_Templates and exported tables to D:\\Meat_Quality_Output . If those folders are missing, the program creates them on drive D:."}, {"title": "Taking images", "url": "imaging.html", "text": "MMQ measures images that have already been stored in folders. One folder should hold the images of one animal. The program reads the folder name into the animal list. Put the animal ID in the information table; that ID is the first column of the exported CSV file. What to prepare Use a cross-section image of the meat sample. The program removes colours outside the meat before measurement and keeps the processed copies in the measure_image folder next to the executable. For ordinary measurement the main window shows the first three images in the folder. If the folder contains more than three images, the program shows a notice. See Measurement . Colour calibration needs a folder with at least two images. A folder with only one image is rejected. See Calibration . Collect a 45-minute sample, a 24-hour sample, or both. Marbling score and intramuscular fat are available only when the measurement time is set to the 24-hour sample. PorkMS, in the browser, accepts JPG and PNG. You can drop a file onto the upload area. See PorkMS . Choose the species and open a template Use Menu File . The species defaults to pig. Choose cattle, sheep or chicken here when that is the sample. Then use New information template . A template picker opens. Saved templates are listed from D:\\Meat_Quality_Templates . Fig. 13. Template picker. Fill in the template If the breed you need is not in the list, type it. Save stores the template so it appears in the picker later. Confirm also stores it and sends the fields into the animal information table on the main window. Fig. 15. Animal information table after a template is applied. Fields on the animal information table: Field Role ID Animal identifier. Written as the first column of the CSV file (the ear-tag / ID column described in the manual). Slaughterhouse Plant or laboratory name. Operator 1, 2 and 3 People who made the measurement. Scanner brand and model Device used to capture the image. Breeding company Farm or company that raised the animal. Breed Breed name. Free text is allowed. Sex Sex of the animal. Live weight (kg) Body weight at measurement. L*, a*, b* (general, 45-minute sample) Reference colour values for the 45-minute sample. Meat colour score (NPPC, 6-point) Reference colour score on the NPPC 6-point scale. Marbling score (NPPC, 6-point) Reference marbling score on the NPPC 6-point scale. Intramuscular fat (%) Reference IMF on the general scale. Import image folders Use Menu Tools &gt; Import folder . Select one folder, or hold Ctrl and select several, then confirm. The folders appear in the animal list. The status bar counts imported animals and imported images. Fig. 17. Animal list after folders have been imported. Remove a folder Select a folder and click Delete . Confirm the prompt. Select all followed by Delete removes every imported folder. Deleting a folder also deletes measurement data and pictures that MMQ stored for it. Fig. 19. The animal list after a folder has been deleted. The next step is Parameters , if mode, model and time are not set yet, then Measurement ."}, {"title": "How to use MMQ?", "url": "help.html", "text": "Installation Use a 64-bit Windows 10 or Windows 11 PC that meets the hardware list . Run MMQ Determination Software.exe as administrator. Work in this order Log in , or recover or change the password. Set the mode, the model and the time . Marbling and IMF need the 24-hour setting. Create an information template and import image folders . Calibrate the meat-colour score if you want to. This step can be skipped. Measure one image, a manual batch, or an automatic batch. Export the finished rows to CSV. The main window: animal list, information table, image panes and results. Menu map File — species, new information template, data export. Tools — measurement mode, measurement model, import folder. Measurement time — 45-minute sample or 24-hour sample. Meat-colour calibration — optional colour-score calibration. Help &gt; User guide — opens the packaged user guide. Online marbling For a browser-only fat-ratio grade, use PorkMS instead of the desktop program. Its grades are not NPPC 6-point scores. Five rules from the measurement chapter: If you change the mode, the model or the time after a result has been calculated, MMQ clears that result. Measure the folder again. A folder that has already been measured is shown again, with its saved numbers, when you tick it. Finished folders are shown in red. Delete a folder from the animal list when you want its saved data and pictures removed. Untick Accepted for an image if that image should stay out of the average. The export file marks it."}, {"title": "Log in", "url": "login.html", "text": "Start MMQ Determination Software.exe as administrator. The log-in window has three actions: log in, recover a password, and change a password. Fig. 1. Log-in window. Initial username admin, initial password 1. Log in Type the username and password and click Log in . After a password change, use the new pair. If the username or the password is wrong, the program shows the warning in Fig. 2. The manual's initial account is username admin and password 1 . Fig. 2. Unrecognized username or password. Recover a password Click Recover password on the log-in window. Type the username and click the button that recovers the password for that user. The password is shown only when the username already exists. Fig. 4. Recovery is refused when the username does not exist. Change a password Click Change password on the log-in window. Type the username and confirm the change. Only an existing user can be changed. After a successful change, log in with the new password. Fig. 6. The password is not changed when the username does not exist. The form in the left column of this website repeats that check for the initial account. It does not start the desktop program and it does not change a password on disk."}, {"title": "Parameters", "url": "parameters.html", "text": "After login the main window opens. Three settings are required before a sample is measured: measurement mode, measurement model and measurement time. Fig. 7. Main window. Menus: File, Tools, Measurement time, Meat-colour calibration, Help. The status bar under the four panes reports the species, how many animals and images were imported, the mode, the model, how many animals have been measured, the time setting, and the data path. Defaults on a new session are: species pig, mode single manual measurement, model NPPC, time 45-minute sample, path D:\\Meat_Quality_Output . Measurement mode Use Menu Tools &gt; Measurement mode . The default is Single manual measurement . Single manual measurement. Click one image, then click the single-measurement button. The numbers appear in that image's column. Multiple manual measurement. Select several images, then click the batch-manual button. Multiple automatic measurement. After the folders are imported, tick the folders you want, or click Select all , then click the automatic batch button. A progress window runs until every selected folder is finished. Measurement model Use Menu Tools &gt; Measurement model . The default is the NPPC model. A different model produces different scores. The information table labels meat colour and marbling as NPPC 6-point scores when that model is in use. Measurement time Use Menu Measurement time . The default is 45-minute sample . Choose 24-hour sample when you need the marbling score and the intramuscular fat content. Those two results are not produced on the 45-minute setting. Changing the mode, the model or the time after a folder has been measured clears the numbers for that folder. Measure it again. Continue with Taking images if the template and folders are not loaded yet."}, {"title": "Colour calibration", "url": "calibration.html", "text": "Meat-colour calibration can be skipped. When the program opens it may show a reminder that calibration has not been done. Fig. 20. Reminder to calibrate the meat-colour score. Start a calibration In the animal list, select a folder that contains at least two images. A folder with a single image is rejected (Fig. 21). Then use Menu Meat-colour calibration . MMQ calculates L*, a* and b* and places them in the calibration window. Fig. 22. Calibration window with L*, a* and b* filled in. Type a manual colour score for each image. You choose whether that score is included in the average. Click Overall calibration . The window then shows the mean of the images that received a valid score. Fig. 23. Means after overall calibration. A smaller standard deviation of b (δb) means a more precise colour score. The standard deviation of b is the figure the manual uses to judge how precise the colour-score calculation is. A smaller δb means a more precise colour model. Cumulative calibration You can calibrate one folder, or you can accumulate several folders. Fig. 24. Choose single-folder calibration or cumulative calibration. In cumulative mode the program keeps the result of the first folder. The next folder is calibrated onward from that result, and the window shows the accumulated values. Fig. 25. Cumulative calibration carried from the previous folder. Fig. 26. Result after more than one folder has been accumulated. When calibration is finished, or if you skip it, go on to Measurement ."}, {"title": "Measurement", "url": "measure.html", "text": "Tick one folder in the animal list. MMQ strips non-meat colours from the first three images and shows them in the image panes. Fig. 27. First images of a folder after background removal. If the folder holds more than three images, a notice is shown (Fig. 28). Processed pictures can be inspected in the measure_image folder beside the executable. Single manual measurement Click one preview image, then click Single manual measurement . L*, a*, b* and the colour score are written into that image's column. On the 45-minute setting the marbling and fat cells stay empty. Multiple manual measurement Select more than one preview image, then click Multiple manual measurement . Each selected image is filled in. Multiple automatic measurement Tick the folders you want, or click Select all , then click Multiple automatic measurement . The progress window stays open until the batch ends. The main window then shows the last folder. Click another folder in the list to see its numbers. Finished folders are drawn in red. Fig. 31. Progress while a batch is running. Fig. 32. The batch has finished. Fig. 33. Main window after automatic measurement. Fig. 34. Open any finished folder to see the numbers stored for it. Marbling score and intramuscular fat Switch Measurement time to 24-hour sample , then measure again with any of the three modes. The marbling score and the fat content are filled in. The result columns also record whether each image is accepted. An image that is not accepted is left out of the average and is marked in the export file. Fig. 35. Marbling score and intramuscular fat on the 24-hour setting. Keep these rules in mind: Changing mode, model or time clears results already shown for the folder. Tick a measured folder again and the saved numbers come back. Measured folders use red text. Deleting the folder from the animal list deletes its saved data and pictures. Only accepted images enter the average. Export the red (finished) folders from Export ."}, {"title": "Export", "url": "export.html", "text": "Export writes only folders that have already been measured. In the animal list those folders are shown in red. Other folders are omitted. Use Menu File &gt; Data export . The export window lists finished animals. Clear the tick in front of an animal if that animal should be left out of the file. Click Save export . The file is written to the folder chosen when the information template was saved. The default folder is D:\\Meat_Quality_Output . If that folder does not exist, MMQ creates it on drive D:. Fig. 37. Confirmation after the CSV file has been written. If you close the program without exporting, or you hit the close button by mistake, MMQ asks you to export before it quits (Fig. 38). Fig. 39. Default export location on drive D:. Fig. 40. Opened CSV file. Columns Column 1 is the animal ID typed in the information table (the ear-tag column in the manual). Column 2 is the image number. Further columns hold the image record and the measured values. A remark distinguishes a 45min sample from a 24h sample. The colour-score column notes which scoring model was used. The last row is the average of the accepted images. An image that was not accepted is marked and is not part of that average. Menu Help &gt; User guide opens the packaged guide, the same document as the PDF manual shipped with the program."}, {"title": "PorkMS", "url": "porkms.html", "text": "PorkMS (Pork Marbling Measurement System) is a browser page for pork marbling. It estimates the fat area ratio on a muscle cross-section and turns that ratio into a marbling grade. Open PorkMS and start. No account is required. This grade is not the NPPC 6-point marbling score used by the MMQ desktop program . Use PorkMS when you want the fat-ratio grade below. Use MMQ when you also need L*, a*, b*, an NPPC colour score and intramuscular fat. What the page provides No login. The page can be used as soon as it opens. One image at a time. Import an image, measure it, and the row is stored. History. Results stay in the history panel, newest or oldest according to the time sort on the page, and can be scrolled. CSV export. The history can be downloaded as one CSV file. Trial quota. Each visit allows 100 successful measurements. Ask the developer of PorkMS if you need more. Local processing. Analysis runs in the browser. Images and numbers are not sent to a server. PorkMS keeps the work on your computer. Refreshing or reopening the page resets the trial quota. It also clears a session that lived only in that page. Animal measurement info The form at the top is optional. It does not change the image analysis. Anything you type is included in the CSV file, which makes the sheet easier to trace. Field Example Location Slaughterhouse or laboratory name Operator Person making this measurement Breed Duroc, Landrace, or another breed name Gender Sex of the animal Date Filled with today, and you can edit it Measure one image Fill in the form if you want a paper trail. Location, operator, breed, gender and date are all optional. Import an image. Click the upload area or drop a JPG or PNG file into the dashed box. A preview appears. The × control removes it. Start measurement. The button shows a busy state while the page analyses the file. A successful run uses one trial. Read the history row. The new row shows the time, a thumbnail of the original, a thumbnail of the marked image, the fat ratio and the marbling grade. Export or clear. Export all as CSV downloads the history. Clear all results removes the rows of the current session. The standard-image strip on PorkMS holds six reference pictures, grades 1 to 6. Analyze under a reference picture runs that picture and adds a history row, which is a quick way to check the scale. Large images take longer because the work is done in the browser. Wait until the busy state ends. Loading a file without clicking Start measurement does not use a trial. Trial quota A visit starts with 100 measurements. The remaining count is shown in the banner. Only a successful measurement is counted. The count returns to 100 when the page is refreshed or opened again. At zero, Start measurement is disabled. Contact the PorkMS developer to continue. Marbling grades The grade follows the fat area ratio on the muscle cross-section. Grade Fat area ratio Description 1 ≤ 0.275% No marbling, extremely low fat 2 0.275% – 2.284% Trace marbling 3 2.284% – 5.902% Slight marbling 4 5.902% – 8.703% Moderate marbling 5 8.703% – 12.464% Medium marbling 6 12.464% – 18.045% Modestly abundant marbling 7 18.045% – 23.508% Abundant marbling 8 23.508% – 28.232% Highly abundant marbling 9 28.232% – 33.355% Extremely abundant marbling 10+ &gt; 33.355% Super abundant marbling How a marked image should be read On the result thumbnail, green regions are fat (marbling) found by the page. The remaining area is the muscle region kept after preprocessing. The page separates the sample with Otsu thresholding, filters it in Lab colour space, and uses connected components to keep a valid muscle region. Fat particles are then taken from block-normalised brightness. The fat area ratio is the input to the table above. Desktop colour calibration, folder batching and IMF are described under How to use MMQ , not on this page."}, {"title": "Pig", "url": "pig.html", "text": "Species Pig Pig is the default species in MMQ. The manual was written for pork colour, marbling and intramuscular fat. Choose the 45-minute sample for the early colour reading. Choose the 24-hour sample when you need the marbling score and IMF. PorkMS is the browser tool for pork marbling grades based on fat area ratio. It does not replace the NPPC 6-point scores in the desktop information table. Set the species with Menu File before you create the information template. The steps after that are in Taking images , Parameters and Measurement ."}, {"title": "Cattle", "url": "cattle.html", "text": "Species Cattle Cattle is one of the species listed for the desktop program. Select it from Menu File . The manual does not define a separate score scale for beef. Mode, model, time, calibration and export work as they do for pig. PorkMS grades pork only. Do not send a beef image to PorkMS and read the result as a beef marbling grade. Set the species with Menu File before you create the information template. The steps after that are in Taking images , Parameters and Measurement ."}, {"title": "Sheep", "url": "sheep.html", "text": "Species Sheep Sheep is selected from Menu File . Image folders, the information template and the three measurement modes are shared with the other species. The manual does not publish a sheep-specific marbling table. The browser grades on the PorkMS page are pork fat-ratio grades. Set the species with Menu File before you create the information template. The steps after that are in Taking images , Parameters and Measurement ."}, {"title": "Chicken", "url": "chicken.html", "text": "Species Chicken Chicken is selected from Menu File . Use the same folder layout: one animal in each folder, with the images MMQ will preview. Colour values L*, a* and b* are still the values the desktop program calculates for a loaded image. Intramuscular-fat and marbling wording in the manual is written around pork. Treat a chicken result with that limit in mind, and do not grade chicken fillets with PorkMS. Set the species with Menu File before you create the information template. The steps after that are in Taking images , Parameters and Measurement ."}];
